</nav></div></header><section class="article-hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="crumb"><a href="../thought-leadership">Thought Leadership</a> / Time &amp; Labor Data</div><div class="cat">Time &amp; Labor Data</div><h1>Better Payroll Starts at the Punch: How to Improve Time &amp; Labor Data Before It Reaches Workday</h1><p>Payroll cannot fix source data it never received correctly. The strongest workforce-data strategies prevent avoidable errors at the employee interaction—before they become manager edits, payroll exceptions, costing corrections and support tickets.</p><div class="meta"><span>ZKTeco WFM Insights</span><span>Educational Perspective</span><span>Updated September 2026</span></div></div></section><main class="content-wrap"><article class="article-body"><p class="lead">Payroll is often where bad workforce data becomes visible. It is rarely where the problem began. A missing punch, incorrect labor choice, duplicate transaction, wrong department transfer or unrecorded attestation usually originates much earlier—at the moment an employee interacts with the collection system.</p>
<div class="tl-flow"><div><span>1</span>Employee</div><div><span>2</span>Identity</div><div><span>3</span>Time + Labor Event</div><div><span>4</span>Validation</div><div><span>5</span>Workday / Payroll</div></div>
<h2>Payroll accuracy begins with workforce-data quality</h2><p>Every downstream process depends on the quality of the event captured upstream. If the employee is identified incorrectly, the timestamp is incomplete, the wrong labor context is attached, or a required response is missing, the organization has created an exception that someone must eventually resolve.</p><p>The better question is not simply, <strong>“How efficiently can payroll clean up exceptions?”</strong> It is: <strong>“Which preventable exceptions can we stop or clarify while the employee still has the context to answer?”</strong></p>
<div class="tl-scenario"><div class="eyebrow">Operating example</div><h2>One wrong labor choice can touch four teams</h2><p>An employee works six hours on Job A and two hours on Job B, but never records the transfer. The timecard still contains eight valid hours, so the problem may not look like a timekeeping issue. Later, a supervisor notices the labor distribution is wrong, payroll receives a correction, finance gets revised costing and a support question may be opened to determine why the transfer was missed.</p><p>At the point of work, the correction might have required a few seconds. Downstream, it can become a multi-person process.</p></div>
<h2>What should be controlled at the source?</h2><p>Not every payroll rule belongs on a clock. The goal is not to recreate payroll at the workforce edge. The goal is to place <strong>simple, understandable controls where they prevent predictable data-quality problems.</strong></p>
<div class="tl-grid"><div class="tl-card"><h3>Identity</h3><p>Confirm that the transaction belongs to the correct employee through the credential or authentication method appropriate to the workforce.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>Sequence</h3><p>Help prevent obvious transaction errors such as rapid duplicate punches or invalid punch sequences.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>Labor context</h3><p>Present the job, department, cost center or other labor choice required to make the time meaningful downstream.</p></div><div class="tl-card"><h3>Context and acknowledgment</h3><p>Use targeted prompts or attestations when the organization needs information the system cannot determine automatically.</p></div></div>
<h2>Use defaults when the system already knows the answer</h2><p>Employees should not be asked to make accounting decisions unnecessarily. If a worker normally belongs to one department or job, the collection experience can often default that value and ask the employee only when an exception occurs.</p><p>This matters because every additional choice creates another opportunity for error. Workday itself supports defaulting and restricting worktags in time entry, which reinforces the broader design principle: <strong>present only the labor context needed for the transaction.</strong></p>
<div class="tl-overlook"><div class="eyebrow">What most buyers overlook</div><h2>Data quality is an employee-experience issue</h2><p>Organizations sometimes treat controls and usability as opposites. They do not have to be. The best source controls make the correct action easier: fewer irrelevant choices, immediate feedback, clear instructions and a defined path for legitimate exceptions.</p><p>A control that blocks valid work or confuses employees can create more corrections than it prevents.</p></div>
<h2>Measure the exceptions you are trying to prevent</h2><p>Transaction volume alone tells little about quality. A more useful operating dashboard tracks recurring sources of rework, such as:</p><ul><li>missing punches;</li><li>duplicate events;</li><li>manager timecard edits;</li><li>wrong or missing labor allocations;</li><li>rejected transactions;</li><li>frequent overrides;</li><li>site-specific exception rates.</li></ul><p>If one location consistently generates more corrections than another, the root cause may be workflow design, device placement, training, configuration or data synchronization—not employee behavior.</p>
<h2>Prevent, warn or allow?</h2><table class="tl-table"><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Use when</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Prevent</strong></td><td>The action is clearly invalid and blocking it will not stop legitimate work.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Warn</strong></td><td>The transaction may be unusual but could be legitimate; provide context and allow an authorized path forward.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capture + flag</strong></td><td>The employee must be allowed to work, while the exception is routed for downstream review.</td></tr></table>
<h2>Questions leaders should ask</h2><div class="questions"><ul><li>Which time and labor exceptions consume the most manager or payroll effort?</li><li>Which of those errors are predictable at the point of collection?</li><li>Can the system determine the correct value automatically?</li><li>Which rules should block versus warn?</li><li>How does the employee proceed when the exception is legitimate?</li><li>Can we measure exception rates by site, clock or workflow?</li><li>Can support trace an event from the employee interaction through Workday?</li></ul></div>
<div class="tl-rule"><div class="eyebrow">Decision rule</div><strong>Move preventable errors upstream, but keep legitimate work moving.</strong><p>Use the workforce edge to improve identity, sequence, labor context and immediate feedback. Keep complex payroll calculations and policy ownership in the appropriate system of record.</p></div>
<div class="perspective"><div class="label">Why ZKTeco WFM fits this model</div><h2>Capture cleaner data before Workday has to interpret it.</h2><p>ZKTeco WFM approaches time collection as a workforce-data problem, not simply a punch transaction. Ultima enterprise time clocks provide the purpose-built endpoint, TimeTrack controls the employee interaction, and CirrusDCS supports the Workday-specific data flow. Together, that architecture can help organizations capture the employee, time, labor context and required acknowledgments while the employee is still present and able to correct a mistake.</p><p>That matters because the highest-value control is often the one that prevents an avoidable exception from becoming a downstream correction. Depending on the approved configuration, TimeTrack can support labor selection, transfers, attestations, schedule-aware controls, duplicate-punch controls and clear employee feedback. CirrusDCS then connects those workforce events with Workday while supporting the operational visibility needed to manage the environment.</p><p>The ZKTeco WFM advantage is therefore not one isolated feature. It is the combination of <strong>purpose-built hardware + employee workflow + source controls + Workday integration + implementation and support</strong> in one operating model. The objective is simple: make the original workforce event as trustworthy and usable as practical before it enters the systems that depend on it.</p></div><div class="takeaway"><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p>Payroll cleanup is often a symptom of a problem that began much earlier. The strongest time-and-labor architecture captures the actual event, gives the employee enough context to get it right, prevents obvious errors where appropriate and preserves a clear path for legitimate exceptions. Better payroll data therefore begins at the workforce edge—not in the final payroll review. When the source event is cleaner, every downstream process has a better starting point.</p></div>
